WEBVTT

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:15.320
 Those relationships are first and foremost. Our cooking is all about the farmers, the small producers, the cheese makers, the olive oil producers, the small wineries, etc. That's what the restaurant's about.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:27.070
 I'm always one of the people who's in the background. I'm not a person who's in the foreground. I'm a silent change maker. I'm not one of those people out there on the front lines.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:44.280
 You know, I have so much respect for people who've been out there right in the foreground for so many years. Alice Waters and so many other people who, you know, just have made a huge difference and who have to keep fighting for small farms, fighting against pesticides, all of that.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:54.420
 But yeah, we're definitely part of it. There's absolutely no question. And I feel that we have changed people's perception about how you can eat well.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:05.340
 And I think we continue to change people's perceptions every day when people come in.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.340
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:16.960
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.030
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:30.210
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:39.510
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills. Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:50.350
 Annie Somerville is the executive chef of Green's Restaurant in San Francisco, an innovator of farm-to-table and vegetarian food since 1979.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:59.790
 Green's is owned by the San Francisco Zen Center, buys much of their produce from the Zen Center's Green Gulch Farm, and has an exclusively vegetarian menu.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:06.820
 The restaurant occupies a decommissioned army pier on the San Francisco Bay with views of fog rolling into the water.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.220
 Annie is the author of two cookbooks, Fields of Greens and Everyday Greens.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:18.010
 I've read that you got into cooking at Tassajara.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:21.810
 Were you a cook before that, or what drew you to the kitchen?

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:28.880
 Let me see. I did a little cooking before Tassajara, but never, of course, never professionally.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.660
 And when I was at Tassajara, I was quite a bit younger than I am now.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.020
 And I really, there were a lot of things I liked about cooking at Tassajara.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:44.210
 One was, the kitchen staff at Tassajara had a different schedule than everyone else, so there was a lot of independence there.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:48.700
 And we got to do all kinds of experimental things.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:56.180
 And the other thing was, in the winter when it was really cold, we got to be indoors in the warm kitchen when everyone else was outside working.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.970
 So, it was great to be roasting nuts and doing all kinds of things in there.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:07.910
 To provide a little context, let's talk about Tassajara and what it is and what made that kitchen special.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:14.530
 Wow. Well, let's see. Tassajara is part of the San Francisco Zen Center.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:22.910
 Zen Center has three locations, a city center here at Page and Laguna in San Francisco, Green Gulch Farm and Organic Farm and Meditation Center in Marin County,

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:36.280
 and then Tassajara, which is Zen Center's monastery in the coastal range in a very remote place, which is not so far away, but it's remote and it takes quite a bit of time to get there.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:45.240
 So, in the summer months, Tassajara is open to a summer guest season, which helps support the San Francisco Zen Center.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:50.640
 All the Zen practitioners are there, as well as about 60 guests every day and night.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:04:01.930
 And then, in the winter months, Tassajara is closed for formal meditation periods, strictly meditation and for Zen practitioners.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:09.070
 So, what's so amazing about the Tassajara kitchen is, first of all, it's in a very remote, faraway place.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:16.350
 All kinds of things happen there, storms, floods, fires, and you have to be prepared.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.320
 And it's just a place that people love.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.280
 It's one of those places that's very beautiful.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:23.500
 It's a way to get away from the world.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.530
 And, I guess, it's a Hot Springs resort.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:34.140
 It's an old Indian Hot Springs camping place where the Esalen Indians used to come from the coast.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.320
 And it's just a very, very unusual place.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:44.420
 You know, Tassajara Creek runs through this very narrow valley, so you always hear the sound of the creek.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.350
 People eat in a very relaxed, family-style way.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:50.810
 Meals are served family-style.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:57.510
 There's a lot of pressure on the cooks during the summer guest season because you're not only cooking for the guests three meals a day.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.410
 You're also cooking for about 60 Zen students, as well.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:04.260
 And then, of course, in the winter months, it's a lot quieter.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.300
 You're cooking for the people who are in the meditation retreat.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:11.300
 And it's just a beautiful stone and wooden building.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:13.610
 There's a lot of history there.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.570
 What are you thinking of when you're talking about being prepared for fires?

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:22.030
 Is there a time when you remember that you got stuck out there?

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:29.550
 Well, let's just say I hope I was not the cause of some of these major, we'll just say, emergencies.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:35.220
 But we had, you know, we are very, California's prone to fires and floods.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:47.840
 So when I was at Tassajara in the 70s, we had the Marble Cone Fire, which was a fire started by a lightning strike out in Big Sur that just burned into Tassajara.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.560
 So we had to evacuate Tassajara.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.400
 Few people were able to stay.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:57.130
 Fire didn't actually reach Tassajara, but, or came, but we were prepared for it.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:04.600
 After that, of course, the watershed, and this was before I understood what the word watershed meant, the entire watershed had been burned.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:06.920
 So, of course, it was a very rainy year.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:21.650
 We flooded, and as the new, this was my first time being the head chef, and I was told, or head cook, I was told to prepare for the road washing out because it was very likely that the creek would overflow and we would flood.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:23.130
 And I did.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:27.850
 I didn't know anything about buying, though, so they said, just order a lot of dry goods.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:28.870
 So, of course, I did.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:31.930
 I ordered about five times too many dry goods, more than we needed.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:34.840
 And then that happened.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:35.740
 We had the flood.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:36.600
 We survived that.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.200
 And then in the spring, we still had a lot of those dry goods down in our food storage area.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:47.270
 And then in the spring, we had a structural fire, big fire, which burned down our meditation hall and the food storage area.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:53.880
 So, I learned something about ordering, which is, even if you need to order a lot for a rainy day, don't order too much.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:58.550
 And you've been ordering really fresh food ever since.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:06:59.430
 Is that true?

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:06.170
 I think one of the most unique things about greens is its relationship with Green Gulch and a lot of other farms around.

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:11.050
 And I know you're well known for being committed to those relationships.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:17.880
 Can you talk about how you formed some of those relationships and also how that influences the cooking here?

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:22.390
 Well, first, I'll say that those relationships are first and foremost.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:24.530
 That's what forms up our cooking.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:34.880
 Our cooking is all about farmers, the small producers, the cheese makers, the olive oil producers, the small wineries, et cetera.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:36.360
 That's what the restaurant's about.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:47.400
 And then just over the many years, we, I, we have cultivated many, many, many relationships with farmers that deliver directly to greens.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.500
 Of course, we are, as I say, connected at the hip to Green Gulch because Green Gulch is our family.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:57.800
 And people were farming at Green Gulch well before greens came along.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:58.320
 Let me see.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:04.120
 They started farming at Green Gulch in maybe the early 70s, maybe 73, 74, 70, 1975.

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:08.740
 And then greens opened in 1979.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:13.490
 So it seemed like a long time, even though now when I recall it, a few years isn't that much.

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:17.770
 Yeah, we just worked with, we're working, I can't even, I mean, many, many farmers.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:24.810
 Warren Weber at Starroot Farms, who was one of the authors of the California Certified Organic, CCOF.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:31.370
 You know, that's very serious legislation for, and one of the strictest for organic principles.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.450
 And so we've worked with Starroot for many, many years.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:36.190
 Green Gulch.

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:37.670
 Many farms in Marin.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.010
 I shop at the Ferry Plaza Farmer's Market twice a week.

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:42.750
 So we're connected to many farmers there.

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:45.950
 Farms that wouldn't otherwise deliver to the restaurant.

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:47.050
 Some of them do.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:54.240
 But mostly I'm picking up berries and fresh beans and just ingredients.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:58.440
 Like right now we're kind of, we're fall, but it's early November.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:01.100
 It's beautiful and warm and there's still berries.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:02.000
 There's still fresh beans.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.020
 There's still, there's still some really good tomatoes.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:13.050
 So I still, I'm hanging on to those late, late season as we're moving into the early season of like fall and winter.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.180
 Not everyone was doing this.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:20.080
 There wasn't really an established model for this when you first came to greens.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:20.880
 Is that right?

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:30.100
 I mean, it sounds like a magical time in the late 70s and early 80s for innovations and connecting good food to a good food movement and good food system.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:32.730
 Well, that's true.

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:39.040
 Um, I would say when I came to greens, I mean, I didn't know what I was doing.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.700
 I don't think, I think a lot of us didn't really know what we were doing at like the start of anything, something starting up.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:46.580
 It's just, you're just doing things.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:52.140
 And, um, and then later on it either forms up or it, or it goes away, right?

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:55.460
 If it's not solid, stable, it doesn't have a foundation.

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:56.480
 It just fades out.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:01.210
 So, yeah, it was, when I came to greens, it was very exciting.

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:09.200
 Uh, I had never worked in a restaurant kitchen before, even though I had cooked at Tassajara and, uh, the other Zen Center kitchens.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:15.300
 I just was really unprepared for the intensity of working in a busy restaurant kitchen.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:21.870
 And, you know, really here, most everyone was just doing it for the first time.

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:27.410
 So, it was, it was, let's just say, it had its own level of organization, but it was pretty, pretty crazy.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:32.530
 Um, and of course, it's all vegetables and it was an exciting time.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:35.190
 And at that time, there were just a few farms.

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.930
 Of course, Green Gulch, which we could get a little bit of produce from.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:42.710
 There just weren't very many farms right here that were doing what is happening today,

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:49.250
 which is just making organic, sustainably grown produce and ingredients just accessible the way they are.

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:58.180
 So, we actually had a buyer who would go up to the Delta, pick up tomatoes, pick up a lot of the fresh summer crops,

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:02.980
 which we could get through farmers who were actually, Debra Madison, who was then our chef,

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:06.980
 was connected to because she had grown up in Davis and she just knew all these people.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:09.080
 So, or knew of them.

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:14.740
 And then we had a wonderful, at that time, as we still do, wonderful connection to Chez Panisse.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.680
 So, there was sort of this sort of sense of, I guess, being on a big adventure.

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:23.520
 With others on the same adventure, but new to the adventure.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:34.710
 And then, what's it been like to have a kind of model for food and a food movement grow up around some of these ideas that you were working on back then?

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:45.390
 Well, I think, you know, when you're part of history, you're just living it and you don't necessarily realize that you're doing what you're doing.

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:47.150
 You're just doing it, right?

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:48.190
 Which is what we've done.

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:54.370
 So, it's all formed up beautifully, is what I want to say.

00:11:54.530 --> 00:12:01.170
 I think that the real challenges are, can small farmers continue to do what they do?

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.320
 You know, let's see the 70s, the 80s.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.200
 You know, people could afford to buy land.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:09.860
 You know, the whole question of being able to buy land.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:16.100
 You know, can small farmers find the help that they need, just with all the issues around immigration?

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:20.220
 And, you know, it's just, it's very rigorous work.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:21.760
 Restaurants are hard work.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.180
 Farming is much harder work.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:28.020
 And also, just with the, just like the unpredictable, well, California's always been a very unpredictable place.

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.860
 Climate is even more unpredictable.

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:35.430
 So, I think that, you know, we just feel like we're part of it.

00:12:35.530 --> 00:12:39.110
 And I'm happy to be one of the early chefs.

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:45.390
 I think I'm definitely one of, we'll just say, the more senior chefs shopping at the farmer's market on Tuesdays and Saturdays.

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:48.440
 I'm happy to be part of it.

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:59.880
 When I worked here, I think we figured out, I think it was nine years ago, it was, it struck me that your kitchen is definitely the happiest kitchen to work in that I ever worked in.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:03.390
 Is that something that you put a lot of thought into?

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:09.230
 Well, I think that it's a lot of things.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:17.310
 One, I feel like there's a certain thing in the bones of Greens, or just it's in the walls here, which is we started out as a community.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:21.290
 Because when Greens first opened, everyone working here was part of the Zen Center.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:27.050
 So, there's a sense of that, because people, new people who come here often say this place feels like family.

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:30.650
 And I know a lot of restaurants do feel like family, and a lot do not.

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:34.430
 We're kind of a gentle place to work.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:40.250
 We're not, it's not, we're not in, we're not hardcore the way many restaurants are.

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:43.730
 I think also the fact that it's a lot of things.

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:45.530
 We're owned by the San Francisco Zen Center.

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:47.910
 So, that's one great thing.

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:54.070
 We're supporting Zen Buddhist practitioners and what, and their pursuits of making the world a better place.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:56.790
 We cook vegetables.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:58.050
 There are no animal carcasses.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:13:59.550
 That's a great thing.

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:06.630
 You know, and also, I always notice that no matter how clean a kitchen is, the first thing I notice when I go into other kitchens is I smell animals.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:09.930
 You know, you smell the bones, you smell the carcasses, the meat, the fat, the whatnot.

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:12.030
 So, we don't have any of that here.

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:15.110
 So, we haven't, no animals have died because of greens.

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:19.490
 And then, also, it's a very spacious place.

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:20.850
 It's a very large restaurant.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.990
 I don't think anyone would ever design a restaurant quite like this today.

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:29.190
 We go back to, probably this restaurant was designed in 1977, 78.

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:30.130
 It's a long time ago.

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:34.170
 So, the way the space was laid out, it's different than the way people do it now.

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:35.930
 The kitchen is very spacious.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.600
 Lots of light, lots of fresh air.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:39.680
 We're right on the bay.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:41.420
 We're in Fort Mason Center.

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:44.100
 That, unto itself, is its own environment.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:52.700
 And we're really on the edge of the city, which, as it's good and bad points, it's not so great from a public transportation perspective, but it's just a great place to work.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:57.340
 And we just have people, I think the place brings out the best in people.

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:03.120
 One of the people we interviewed for the last season is Saru Jairaman, and she leads the Restaurant Opportunity Center.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:16.940
 And she really got us thinking a lot about how labor works in restaurants and how restaurants can be a dismal place to work, but also a really great place to make a living.

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:29.970
 So, what are some of the things that you think about and that you try to do to make this a good place to work and make a living in such a, in kind of a hard time to be a working person in San Francisco?

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:42.460
 Well, it is a hard time to be a working person in this world, I think, and especially so many pressures in the Bay Region, housing, the high cost of living.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:50.280
 I realize, I never think about this, but for all these years, I have been, I know for myself, I have been eating at Greens.

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:55.470
 I've been eating all this food, and I eat Greens leftovers, and, you know, it's a good, healthy lifestyle.

00:15:56.750 --> 00:16:01.070
 So, one thing that's great, we're very generous with feeding our staff.

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:01.850
 That's one thing.

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:07.950
 So, for people who don't earn a lot of money, it makes a difference to be able to know that you're going to, you can eat.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.190
 You don't have to think about your meal.

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:14.430
 You can take leftovers home to your family if you want to or need to.

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:22.450
 I think for people, one of the most important things is because so much work is repetitious.

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:26.530
 Restaurant work is definitely repetitious, and it can become monotonous.

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:27.690
 It can be boring.

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:32.350
 You know, the most important thing to me is for people to feel appreciated.

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:37.490
 I make it a point to say hello to every person who works here every day.

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:39.190
 I learn people's names.

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:42.560
 It's not always easy to remember everyone's name.

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:48.500
 We have much more turnover now than we have had in the past just because of the times that we live in.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:50.550
 But I think those things are important.

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:58.810
 And I think that generally all of our managers respect our staff.

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:00.110
 Respect is huge.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:07.670
 And I think that people who work here are happy for the most part.

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.990
 And also, this place elevates people.

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:12.580
 It's a beautiful location.

00:17:13.240 --> 00:17:16.480
 We treat everyone the same here, no matter who you are.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:20.340
 And we treat, basically, we try to treat our staff the same way we treat our customers.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:28.720
 And good things come out of that, of people not feeling oppressed or repressed or people feeling that.

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:30.920
 And we follow all the labor practices.

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:32.480
 You know, we do all the things.

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:35.580
 We make every effort to be a very good employer.

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:39.630
 And I think it works well for us.

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:44.250
 And also, there's just something nice about all this beautiful fresh produce.

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:47.810
 You know, there's just something about the ingredients that come in the door.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:51.010
 You know, these and knowing the people who make the deliveries.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:52.830
 You know, it's all very personal.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:57.170
 So, it does have, hopefully, the good qualities of family.

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:00.400
 That sounds true from when I worked here.

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:05.990
 One of the things that Greens is famous for is, as you mentioned, cooking vegetarian food

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:10.390
 and kind of making vegetables the star of the meal.

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:12.720
 Was that always received so well?

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:16.900
 Was that a hard sell to make in the early days of Greens?

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.210
 We'll just say yes and no.

00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:23.290
 The first is because what we were doing was so different.

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:28.270
 We became very well known right off the bat.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:35.140
 And so, that was very much in our favor because it isn't always easy to convince someone who's

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:40.700
 used to, you know, traditional American fare, at least in those days.

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:45.540
 That's not the case now because vegetables do play a starring role on so many plates.

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:51.440
 It's not always easy to convince people that they're going to feel satisfied and they're

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:53.660
 going to have enough to eat and they're going to be really happy eating it.

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.480
 So, that was, yeah, that's been a bit of a win over.

00:18:57.020 --> 00:19:05.230
 But I think also as people become more aware of all the things like diet, relieving stress,

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:16.110
 you know, just getting, you know, having a better lifestyle, eating well, get, cutting down on animal protein, not making it the center of the plate, but like, you

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:20.370
 could make it a side or, or you can have a wonderful, you don't have to be a vegetarian to eat at Greens.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:23.150
 You can just enjoy a vegetarian meal.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:28.350
 Or some people are, you know, vegan for two days a week or whatever it may be.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:32.470
 I always feel like we're a place where we're a pretty egalitarian place.

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:37.450
 A lot of different types of people come here, whether it's people who are coming because there's

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:43.090
 an event in Fort Mason, whether it's vegetarians, someone who's never been here before, vegetarians

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:46.290
 bring their families here because they feel like this is one place where I think my family

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:47.650
 can actually have a good meal.

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:51.770
 We do a lot of private parties, weddings, all kinds of events.

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:59.690
 Sometimes it's hard to assure the family of the vegetarian bride or groom that they are going

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:02.410
 to have a wonderful time here and they're going to feel okay about paying the bill.

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:07.650
 So, yeah, but still, even in these days, some people do need to be convinced.

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:15.090
 But my goal for our food is that it's delicious, it's fresh, people look at the plate, it's so

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:15.450
 beautiful.

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:19.090
 You know, you already feel great just as it arrives at the table.

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:22.270
 And then when you finish your meal, you feel really good.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:24.850
 You don't feel like you've eaten too much.

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:26.230
 It's not too rich.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:29.290
 We don't slip in a lot of secret ingredients.

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:33.810
 And that's my hope, is that people will feel very good.

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:40.320
 They will know that they've eaten very good, locally sourced, fresh food that has been well

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:40.700
 prepared.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:42.840
 And they're going to feel great afterwards.

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:43.680
 And they'll come back.

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:48.570
 Maybe, can you give us an example of something that you're cooking now or something that maybe

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:50.330
 is more recent on the menu?

00:20:50.710 --> 00:20:53.610
 Because there's this wonderful simplicity to the food you make.

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:56.610
 But there's also kind of something that feels, it feels up to date.

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:58.470
 You know, it feels contemporary.

00:20:58.730 --> 00:20:59.470
 Well, let's see.

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:01.130
 I mean, there's so many things right now.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:04.300
 And also, I'll say that I'm a collaborator.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:08.520
 So I love it when our chefs have good ideas.

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:12.570
 And we have, for instance, our lunch chef is from El Salvador.

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:15.410
 And she's a great home cook.

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:17.790
 She has a beautiful garden at home.

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:19.330
 You know, she's an inspiring person.

00:21:19.990 --> 00:21:22.310
 And so, you know, this isn't so recent.

00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:26.730
 But we now have pupusas, one of our main first courses.

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:27.970
 And they're delicious.

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:29.250
 We change them seasonally.

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:31.070
 But we just, they're great.

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:32.930
 You know, it's made with, it's masa dough.

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:34.550
 So it's gluten-free.

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:36.350
 So we can take care of that.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:38.010
 Scratch that right off the list.

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:39.670
 People who are allergic to gluten can eat them.

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:43.350
 We put different fillings in them, make them kind of spicy and nice.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:49.490
 The pupusas are filled with summer squash, cheddar cheese, serrano chilies, pumpkin seeds,

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:51.870
 sometimes squash blossoms, not now.

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:59.570
 And then we're serving that with a beautiful yellow tomato salsa, nice and spicy,

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:04.010
 slices of avocado, which come from the farmer's market, and then beautiful pickled vegetables.

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:07.630
 And for me, that'd be something that I would have as my main course.

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:11.150
 A lot of people have that as a first course, and you can share it as well.

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:12.810
 That's just something great.

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:18.590
 Another dish that I really like, which has become one of our seasonal regulars,

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:20.750
 is a stuffed poblano chili.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:22.590
 And I know that we can have those so many ways.

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:26.710
 They're not, they are, poblanos are fire roasted, not fried.

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:34.570
 And then filled with quinoa, at this time, we're still using corn, chilies, goat cheese,

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:38.990
 and that unto itself, and then serve on a bed of really nice saucy beans.

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:45.970
 So you get the complete protein of not only corn and beans, one of the traditional forms of protein,

00:22:46.310 --> 00:22:51.870
 but also you get quinoa, you get some yummy goat cheese, salsa, avocado, a little creme fraiche.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:53.230
 For me, that's happiness.

00:22:54.820 --> 00:23:00.460
 I have a big question for you, Annie, and that's, do you see yourself as part of a food movement?

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:06.200
 Do you see your work or yourself as part of a bigger food movement?

00:23:07.330 --> 00:23:15.940
 Well, I think I am, and Greens is, although I'm always one of the people who's in the background.

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:17.860
 I'm not a person who's in the foreground.

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:21.220
 Like, I'm a silent change maker.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:24.820
 I'm not one of those people out there on the front lines.

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:28.320
 But, yeah, we're definitely part of it.

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:29.660
 There's absolutely no question.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:35.970
 And I feel that we have changed people's perception about how you can eat well,

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:40.730
 and I think we continue to change people's perceptions every day when people come in.

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:49.620
 And the other thing I think is interesting about Greens is that I would say that the majority of our diners are not vegetarian.

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:55.300
 They like coming here because the food is delicious, and it's really fresh.

00:23:55.300 --> 00:23:58.480
 They know they can have a good meal here, and it's just a beautiful setting.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:03.180
 It's just an, and also it's the kind of place when you walk in the door, it makes you feel good.

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:07.210
 You know, I always say 50% of the job is done when people walk in and see the view.

00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:13.470
 So we have 50% to do, which is to make something delicious and make people feel good by giving them great service.

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:15.110
 Yep, so we're part of it.

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:22.210
 You know, but I'm not a, you know, I have so much respect for people who've been out there right in the foreground for so many years,

00:24:22.210 --> 00:24:28.360
 Alice Waters and so many other people who, you know, just make, have made a huge difference

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:38.540
 and who have to keep fighting for small farms, you know, GMOs, fighting against pesticides, all of that.

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:49.010
 Because that's, it's to me unbelievable that we can be, for instance, ruining this beautiful state for export.

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:56.900
 We are depriving ourselves of water because of the relationship that corporate farming has to lawmakers.

00:24:57.520 --> 00:24:59.920
 It's just crazy and unfortunate.

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:08.070
 Well, let's talk a little bit more about what are some of those, you know, a lot has changed in the way that people think about food.

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:11.400
 What are some of those challenges that we have right now?

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:13.470
 You mentioned the cost of buying land.

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:15.270
 You mentioned kind of running out of water.

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:22.890
 And so I guess maybe if we could talk about some more of those challenges now and how a restaurant,

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:28.570
 and especially a well-known restaurant, gets to engage with some of those tough issues.

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:31.120
 Well, there are a lot of challenges.

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:35.900
 And also, particularly, San Francisco has always been a gold rush city, you know.

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:37.860
 So we're in another gold rush.

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:42.230
 And I feel everybody knows that we have a housing crisis.

00:25:42.430 --> 00:25:43.550
 I think everyone knows.

00:25:43.650 --> 00:25:50.750
 If they don't know, they need to know that we have a housing crisis and that we are also a service-oriented city, you know.

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:55.710
 But not just people who work in restaurants and hotels and all the service industries.

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:57.510
 I mean, teachers can't even live here.

00:25:57.510 --> 00:26:10.050
 People who are solid, middle-class citizens are fearful of being booted out of their rent-controlled apartments that they've lived in for 20 to 30 years.

00:26:10.570 --> 00:26:13.750
 So it's a scary time in terms of housing.

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:21.450
 And, you know, we're a city that prides itself on diversity, all kinds of people can be here, artists, et cetera.

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:27.460
 And we're, not that all of it's disappearing, but I feel like we're on the verge of losing.

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:31.580
 We have lost so much, we're on the verge of losing more if we don't do something about that.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:35.800
 Of course, you know, sometimes things happen.

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:37.360
 Earthquakes.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:39.120
 Not that I wish for another earthquake.

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:43.380
 But, you know, it kind of shakes things up and suddenly a lot of people leave and guess what?

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:44.660
 Housing situation changes.

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:51.780
 But that's a big challenge for us because the people that we depend on to be able to do this work cannot afford to live here.

00:26:52.120 --> 00:26:59.070
 So, fortunately for us, many of our people are established and they are in stable housing for now.

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:01.030
 You know, you just never know.

00:27:01.150 --> 00:27:05.430
 But it's very difficult for the people who have to get across a bridge.

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:07.230
 They have a long commute on BART.

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:10.210
 BART isn't running late enough at night.

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:11.450
 You know, all of these things.

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:12.750
 The BART cars are too full.

00:27:12.910 --> 00:27:14.130
 There aren't enough buses.

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:15.430
 We need to have more ferries.

00:27:15.910 --> 00:27:22.440
 You know, all of these things are major concerns that I would say most business operators have.

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:26.080
 In the Bay region, not just San Francisco.

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:29.500
 In terms of water, that is a crisis.

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:36.290
 You know, we have promised more water to corporate farming than there is.

00:27:36.430 --> 00:27:37.330
 We simply don't.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:39.130
 The state does not produce that amount of water.

00:27:39.310 --> 00:27:40.250
 That's a big problem.

00:27:40.930 --> 00:27:48.300
 And also, I think that, you know, as we know, our climate is changing right now.

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:50.690
 Weather is unpredictable.

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:52.270
 The earth is warming.

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:59.730
 These are all things to be concerned about in terms of how we make better, how we start learning to recycle water, use less water.

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:05.020
 There's plenty of water to go around if we just use it the way it should be used.

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:12.450
 And we don't promise it to growers who are growing on a huge corporate level, growing crops that are going to be exported.

00:28:12.670 --> 00:28:13.970
 That doesn't make any sense to me.

00:28:14.830 --> 00:28:15.840
 So we have that.

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:16.460
 We have housing.

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:17.660
 We have personnel.

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:18.860
 Oh, also people.

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:20.660
 Just finding people, you know.

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:30.240
 And also one thing that has really changed even in the last 10 years is I think, you know, all the idealistic people who said, I'm going to work in a restaurant.

00:28:30.540 --> 00:28:31.480
 I'm going to be a chef.

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:34.360
 That's changed a lot because it is really hard work.

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:36.440
 A lot of people have moved on.

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:39.520
 They found that it's maybe, I think I'll go back to school.

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:42.780
 I think I'll get my degree, you know.

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:47.010
 I'll become an advocate for food safety, you know, that sort of thing.

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:52.590
 So it's a very different time in terms of how we run restaurants.

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:55.170
 And we have a wonderful staff now.

00:28:55.410 --> 00:29:01.310
 The majority of our staff is, excuse me, English is the second language, if at all.

00:29:01.750 --> 00:29:03.090
 Spanish is the first language.

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:11.240
 And for a restaurant that started out being part of the San Francisco Zen Center, everyone working here was an Anglo.

00:29:11.930 --> 00:29:15.350
 And now a person such as myself, I am in the minority.

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:17.910
 I am, like, on the way out.

00:29:18.070 --> 00:29:21.130
 You know, it's basically Spanish rules the day.

00:29:21.630 --> 00:29:23.710
 And we've got wonderful people.

00:29:23.810 --> 00:29:28.190
 And one of the great things here is people from different countries seem to be able to work well together.

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:30.870
 And in a lot of environments, that doesn't work.

00:29:30.950 --> 00:29:35.430
 You just stick with people from Mexico or Guatemala or El Salvador.

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:37.490
 But we have kind of a good mix here.

00:29:37.790 --> 00:29:46.140
 When I worked here, I spoke a lot of Mayan because I just learned enough to make some dumb jokes and say a couple of things.

00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:48.980
 But, yeah, there's a lot of indigenous languages spoken in the kitchen, too.

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:49.980
 Yes, definitely.

00:29:50.520 --> 00:30:03.020
 And also, for me, realizing that, you know, a lot of our folks in the kitchen may never have, you know, they speak Spanish, but they speak, they came from a country that had a different dialect.

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:08.330
 So, you know, reading comprehension, these are all big things, really big things.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:15.390
 I think the hardest thing is making it possible for people to sustain themselves in this work.

00:30:16.090 --> 00:30:19.510
 And a lot of people have just most people have to work two jobs.

00:30:20.270 --> 00:30:21.490
 It's just the way it is.

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:43.870
 So, besides making it a great place to work, is there a way that a restaurant like this gets to bring some visibility to the struggles or to, you know, I'm thinking about the ways that, you know, a lot of what we value in food really has changed culturally over the years because of restaurants like Greens.

00:30:43.870 --> 00:30:56.200
 And are there ways that restaurants like Greens get to be advocates for or be models for ways that can support the – it's such a people-intensive business, right?

00:30:56.540 --> 00:30:56.960
 Yes, it is.

00:30:57.200 --> 00:31:04.000
 I think for us, you know, we're not out on the front lines of political movements.

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:20.180
 Our way of doing it is to provide really good, steady employment for people and to be a good employer and to, you know, give people the things that they receive in a good environment, you know.

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:22.380
 So that's our way of doing it.

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:29.600
 And I think, you know, to know that your employer is actually – your paycheck is going to be – you're going to get your paycheck on time.

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:30.520
 These are basics.

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:33.260
 But a lot of businesses do not follow these rules.

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:37.960
 You know, we pay our staff over time, generous with vacations.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:41.560
 I mean, we follow all the rules and then we do more for people.

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:44.180
 So that's kind of – that's how we set the model.

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:50.900
 And then I do believe that making it a good environment is a great thing.

00:31:51.520 --> 00:31:53.620
 And I also know that people pass through.

00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:55.460
 You know, it's just – that's just the deal.

00:31:56.020 --> 00:31:57.640
 Restaurants are – you know, it's known.

00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:03.370
 People – a lot of people work through getting their college degree or whatever it is.

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:07.950
 You're working here while you're taking some classes so you can move on to the next thing.

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:10.450
 So then you can go to college, whatever it may be.

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:14.090
 And when people go, I feel like I'm never upset.

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:15.950
 It's just time for people to move on.

00:32:16.010 --> 00:32:19.870
 And hopefully we'll find a qualified person to replace that person who's moved on.

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:26.010
 And I always feel good when a person gets to move on and they're making a big step forward in their life.

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:27.570
 And I encourage that.

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:29.650
 And I hope for that for everyone who works here.

00:32:30.500 --> 00:32:34.080
 A lot of people come here to cook and learn at this kitchen.

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:35.780
 Or at least that was true when I worked here.

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:40.330
 A lot of people were here explicitly because they wanted the experience of working here.

00:32:40.430 --> 00:32:43.220
 And then they go on to cook great things.

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:47.040
 Are there any examples of that that you –

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:49.600
 Well, right now I feel we're really –

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:51.200
 you know, the kitchen's gone through a lot of changes.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:59.730
 It's – again, it's hard to find all those people that you're talking about.

00:32:59.990 --> 00:33:01.570
 Mostly that's not happening here now.

00:33:01.770 --> 00:33:02.390
 Mostly not.

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:06.620
 Mostly now we have people who need jobs and we train.

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:09.880
 However, we – every once in a while we're very lucky.

00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:15.780
 So, for instance, right now we have a person who was a culinary student here from CIA.

00:33:16.180 --> 00:33:17.360
 That's another interesting thing.

00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:23.230
 All of these culinary students used to come to the Bay Area because it was such a great place to learn.

00:33:23.550 --> 00:33:26.190
 They can't afford to do it now because who can afford the housing?

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:30.910
 You cannot afford to come and do a low-paid culinary internship or externship here.

00:33:31.130 --> 00:33:32.230
 So that's really changed.

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:35.730
 However, we did have a lovely person who came last year.

00:33:36.310 --> 00:33:38.900
 She went back to culinary school to finish up.

00:33:38.900 --> 00:33:41.600
 And then she got in touch with us and wanted to return.

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:44.540
 So she's got a lot of – and she's a vegetarian.

00:33:45.090 --> 00:33:47.150
 She specifically wants to work at greens.

00:33:47.650 --> 00:33:48.250
 So we have her.

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:52.940
 We have another lovely woman who just has a ton of creative ideas.

00:33:53.220 --> 00:33:55.160
 So these are good things for the kitchen.

00:33:55.360 --> 00:34:00.180
 And we've recently had some other wonderful people who just have contributed very, very good ideas.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:06.320
 And they kind of get greens and kind of understand what we're trying to do

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:12.000
 and how they can, how should I say, enhance our menu by coming up with some new dishes.

00:34:13.330 --> 00:34:14.370
 Yeah, that's wonderful, Annie.

00:34:14.450 --> 00:34:16.470
 And I think that's all the questions I have.

00:34:16.570 --> 00:34:21.740
 Is there anything else you'd like to talk about before we sign up?

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:23.560
 Is there something I didn't ask you about?

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:33.410
 No, I really think the most important thing is I just really hope that our farmers and our small producers can keep doing what they're doing.

00:34:33.410 --> 00:34:35.320
 I really do have hopes for that.

00:34:36.060 --> 00:34:43.820
 And that, you know, that there's another – that this next generation of inspired people who have good ideas

00:34:43.820 --> 00:34:52.880
 and have good, sound environmental ideas and feel for the planet and take ownership of the planet

00:34:52.880 --> 00:35:01.270
 can take on the task of, you know, growing good food and, you know, making it available to others

00:35:01.270 --> 00:35:05.680
 in a way that's – in a sustainable way and in a way that sustains their lives as well.

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:08.090
 Wonderful, Annie.

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:08.730
 Thanks so much.

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:09.690
 I really appreciate it.

00:35:09.770 --> 00:35:10.190
 Thank you.

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:11.250
 Thank you.

